My mobile does not recognize SIM card: how to solve it step by step
When you change your mobile device, or when you change your SIM card, the terminal often does not recognize it. If you mobile does not recognize SIM card, It can be for several causes that must first be analyzed to know how to proceed to solve the problem.
2 Mobile does not recognize SIM card: possible solutions
2.2 SIM tray inserted incorrectly
2.8 The network is misconfigured
The most common is that the Android mobile or tablet shows a message on the screen warning that your mobile does not recognize the SIM card. This is done by error messages on the table:
Mobile does not recognize SIM card: possible solutions
You must follow these steps knowing well what you are doing or you could damage the mobile or the SIM card. Also, always do it by taking all precautions and backups to avoid accidents or losing data.
Badly inserted card One of the most frequent causes when you have changed your mobile or acquired a new SIM that is put in for the first time is not having placed it correctly.
Now check that it is properly inserted in the slot. And never force these elements, as it should enter without exerting pressure.
Modern mobiles use a card where the SIM (single or dual) and / or the microSD memory card usually go. When it is inserted, even if it has been done correctly, sometimes it does not insert all the way and that causes no contact.
Check the gold contacts of the SIM and the mobile.
Insert the SIM again and see if the problem persists.
Correctly insert another SIM card that you have at your fingertips, from another mobile, prepaid, etc.
In these cases, it is either sent to the technical service for repair or it is replaced by another mobile.
Once all the possible physical problems have been checked in the previous steps, the next thing is to check other software glitches that may also be causing your mobile not to detect a SIM card. Sometimes, due to specific errors in the operating system, the SIM card is not being detected although everything is correct.
One of the most frequent things is that SIM card is not active. To check:
Check in SIM Status that it is correct. Or in SIM cards and mobile networks.
If the Network Operators option is available, find the right one and select yours.
Another common problem that can have symptoms similar to when a mobile does not detect a SIM card is that the APN settings are incorrect.
Select the correct APN or click on New APN to proceed to enter the provider's details. Of course, you will need to know them, so you will probably have to contact him.
Release the buttons and you can now use volume +/- to navigate the menu and select Wipe Cache Partition.
In extreme cases, when none of the above works, try to make a backup or backup of your data, and then proceed to make a hard reset to leave the mobile as it came from the factory and try to see if it was a malicious app or other system problem that was causing this problem. For it:
Now press the Volume + button and the power button simultaneously. Hold down until Android Recovery Mode appears.
Once checked, press the power button to run. Wait for the process to finish and you will see that your mobile restarts as it came from the factory (you must configure, install all the apps, data ...).
Eg some things you sure remember you did with those devices and that now sounds almost ridiculous. Here are the 10 most relevant things you would no longer do with a smartphone:
1 Not charging the battery for days or weeks
3 Save contacts by hand
7 Remove and put the antenna, or extend it
Many of today's mobile devices only have autonomy for a few hours. It is true that they have gained in performance and functionality, but they have also become authentic battery suckers.
Long periods of time without having to charge the mobile. And no, don't think that they were very high-capacity batteries, but those phones had very basic electronics and didn't need as much energy.
If you buy a smartphone, even if it is mid-range or low-end (much more so if it is a high-end one that can reach €1000), surely the second thing you do is buy a case or screen protector.
Now contacts, among other things, are synchronized with the cloud and are automatically saved to have them on any device. Or, you can also export them to a VCF file and easily transfer them to another mobile, PC, make a backup, etc.
In the old mobile phones, the phone numbers could be saved only in the SIM card memory, or in the internal memory, or in both at the same time. If your mobile was lost or damaged, you lost your contacts, if you changed your card or it was damaged too.
Now you can share files at very fast speeds, even with people who are in another country thanks to the Internet. Smartphones have opened up a range of possibilities when it comes to exchanging data, either over the network or via wireless connectivity, and even via USB wiring.
In the old mobiles there was no trace of WhatsApp or Telegram, or any other instant messaging application. The most you had to contact someone via text were SMS, which cost a “grassland” (especially some numbers) and if you had a prepaid card they made it run out in a matter of minutes.
When you wanted to let someone know that you were already waiting for them, or something else, what used to be done was to give them a knock. One call and hang up.
With the Android smartphone you have a multitude of ringtones for calls, for notifications, etc. You can even free download more if you need them, or maybe use the MP3 file of your favorite song as a ringtone.
When did those ads appear on TV where they showed the SMS that you had to send to get the download of said melody?
Remove and put the antenna, or extend it
With these antennas they can have coverage to call or receive calls, as well as for other wireless networks such as LTE data, Bluetooth, etc. Something that makes them much more aesthetic.
Some could be removed and screwed on, others were rigid, and there were even some that could be unfolded and extended like those of the old radios. And be careful not to lose it or break it.
Smartphones have been growing in size (although it is also true that they are becoming thinner, with less frame, and lighter) to incorporate larger and larger screens. Currently, most mobile phones have screens between 5 and 6 inches.
Having the smallest was synonymous with having the most advanced. And it is that from the first bricks that came onto the market, such as the first Motorola DynaTAC 8000X mobile (33×8.9×4.5 cm and 800 grams of weight), little by little they became more and more compact and easy to carry, to make the word mobile made sense.
In the past there were some of the most peculiar fashions and gadgets, something that is not usually present for modern smartphones now. For example, they sold pendants or strips to be able to tie them to a hook that these devices had in order to be able to hang it, etc.
Not only did they decorate, they also prevented them from slipping when placed on certain surfaces.
And lastly, many of the older cell phones used to have flips to protect the keyboard from unwanted keystrokes while you carry them. Then came the clamshell type, which opened and closed with a hinge, and even the sliders, which had a hidden keyboard.
