These days, USB-C hubs are more or less a necessary evil.Many popular laptops have reduced the number of ports they offer, but we still need to plug in more and more accessories.Between the need for dongles for mice and keyboards, hard drives, monitors, and the need to charge headphones and phones, most of us need more — and many different types — of ports.These best USB-C hubs will help you stay connected without slowing you down.
If the hub has USB-A ports, you also need to check which generation they are, as older generation USB-A ports may be too slow for things like transferring files.If it has extra USB-C, you’ll also want to check if it has Thunderbolt compatibility, as this will give you faster speeds.
Why it’s on the list: With three well-spaced USB-A ports, as well as HDMI and SD card slots, this hub is a pretty well-rounded option.
If you’re serious about your monitor setup and want to run multiple monitors at once, this dock is a great option.It has four HDMI 2.0 and four DisplayPort 1.2, both of which support 4K at 60 Hz.This means you can get the most out of your premium monitor while getting plenty of screen real estate.
The downside to all this is that it is very expensive and not travel friendly.If you want to save some money and only use two monitors, there’s also a dual-monitor version that’s a little cheaper.Or, if you travel a lot but still have access to multiple monitors, the Belkin Thunderbolt 3 Dock Mini is a great alternative.
A pluggable USB-C 7-in-1 hub is a great choice for most people, especially those who need to plug in multiple USB-A devices at the same time.You won’t find a travel-friendly hub with more USB-A ports other than larger, more expensive USB-C docks.
Why it’s on the list: This hub works with just about any device, has a long 11-inch cable, and is compact enough to use on the go.
Why it’s here: USB-C hubs can be very expensive, but the Yeolibo 9-in-1 hub is very affordable while still having a huge selection of ports.
There are a total of four USB-A ports on the hub, one of which is the slightly older and slower version 2.0.That means you can plug in many hard drives or dongles for things like a mouse.You also have the option of 85-watt charging via the USB-C PD charging port.For the price, this hub really can’t be beat.
There are many hub options with multiple USB-C ports.These hubs are helpful if you need to expand the number of ports a laptop offers, as many only offer two or three these days (looking at you, Macbooks).
